WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:03.420
The relationship between the United States and China is tense.

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:04.680
Really tense.

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:06.780
Especially when it comes to trade.

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:08.680
It wasn’t always this way.

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:13.280
Decades ago, the United States supported China’s
economic opening, lifting millions of

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:15.340
its people out of poverty.

00:00:15.340 --> 00:00:19.480
This created a huge labor force that helped
make China one of the world’s leading manufacturers

00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:22.320
of everything from electronics to clothing, to steel.

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:25.680
China’s economic boom was good for the U.S. too.

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:28.120
It provided cheap goods for American consumers.

00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:33.700
And it became a massive marketplace for American
soybeans, cars, and many other products.

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:35.300
There were issues, though.

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:40.440
When China joined the World Trade Organization
in 2001, it promised to lower trade barriers

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:43.500
and make its markets fairer for foreign companies.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:48.780
The United States and many other countries
now say China has failed to live up to those promises.

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:53.580
They believe China’s approach to trade violates
international rules and even threatens the

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:55.640
global economic system.

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:01.620
So just how bad are China’s trade practices?

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:03.520
There are big concerns.

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:07.680
China makes it incredibly difficult for outside
companies to do business there.

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:12.100
The government favors its own firms by giving
them billions of dollars in subsidies.

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:16.260
This allows them to export their products
at artificially low prices.

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:21.020
Consumers around the world then buy 
these cheap Chinese products instead of local products.

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:23.820
This drives China’s competitors out of business.

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:29.260
The government also doesn’t always enforce
basic legal protections for foreign companies operating in China

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:34.000
And even if it did enforce the rules, many
find them unfair to outsiders.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:40.200
For instance, foreign firms have often had to create joint ventures with local companies to enter China.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:45.440
In this “partnership,” the foreign firm
is forced to share its technology with the Chinese firm.

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:49.540
This allows the Chinese company to acquire
new technology without paying for it.

00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:53.540
In other cases, Chinese companies straight-up
steal intellectual property.

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:58.200
They have hacked into American computers to
access trade secrets, like blueprints for

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:02.680
a robot used in T-Mobile labs and driverless
car technology from Apple.

00:02:02.689 --> 00:02:06.760
They then use these plans to counterfeit versions
of American products.

00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:11.740
U.S. officials believe the Chinese government
is behind these types of cyber espionage.

00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:18.500
And in the past, the Chinese government has kept its currency weak, giving its exports an added leg up.

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:21.560
Now, none of these complaints are new.

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:26.360
And it’s not just President Donald Trump
who’s attempted to bring China in line.

00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:30.480
Both Democrats and Republicans have pushed
back against China’s trade practices.

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:35.840
The Obama administration tried to get Beijing to do more to stop cyberattacks and intellectual property theft.

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:39.880
And in 2015, the two sides reached a deal
to do just that.

00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:44.500
Both Obama and Trump have used executive powers
to block deals between U.S. and Chinese firms

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:46.860
that might endanger national security.

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:49.900
And they both took complaints to the World
Trade Organization.

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:53.560
Trump has also imposed lots of tariffs on
Chinese goods.

00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:55.640
So has any of it worked?

00:02:55.640 --> 00:03:00.640
China has made some changes, but major economic
conflicts still remain.

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:05.340
And that’s not even mentioning other security
and human rights concerns about China.

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:07.580
Things like censoring the internet.

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:09.540
And monitoring its citizens.

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:12.540
Detaining hundreds of thousands of Uighur Muslims.

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:15.020
And curbing freedoms in Hong Kong.

00:03:15.020 --> 00:03:16.780
The list goes on and on.

00:03:16.780 --> 00:03:21.760
U.S. officials and other experts had hoped
that China’s economic success would lead

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:23.740
it to become more democratic.

00:03:23.740 --> 00:03:26.300
But clearly, that has not happened.

00:03:26.310 --> 00:03:30.420
And getting China to play fair on trade might
not happen either.

